@startuml HttpClient_End_To_End
title HttpClient: end-to-end request flow — from C# code to the NIC and out onto the wire

skinparam backgroundColor #FFFFFF
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign center
skinparam maxMessageSize 220
skinparam noteBackgroundColor #FAEEDA
skinparam noteBorderColor #BA7517
skinparam participantBackgroundColor #E6F1FB
skinparam participantBorderColor #185FA5
skinparam ParticipantPadding 12
skinparam BoxPadding 10

' ===================== PARTICIPANTS =====================
box "User space (ring 3, managed)" #EAF3DE
    actor "Your code" as APP
    participant "HttpClient" as HC
    participant "SocketsHttpHandler\n(connection pool)" as SHH
    participant "SslStream\n(TLS)" as TLS
end box

box "Kernel space (ring 0, privileged)" #FCEBEB
    participant "Socket API\n(syscall layer)" as SOCK
    participant "TCP/IP stack\n(transport + network)" as STACK
    participant "NIC driver\n(kernel module)" as DRV
end box

box "Hardware" #F1EFE8
    participant "NIC\n(physical chip)" as NIC
end box

participant "Remote API server" as SRV

' ===================== ROLES =====================
note over APP
  ROLE: Application layer.
  Thinks only in HTTP terms — URL,
  method, headers, JSON body.
  Zero awareness of sockets/ports/bytes.
end note

note over HC
  ROLE: Thin managed wrapper.
  Stores handler ref, timeout,
  cancellation. Delegates ALL
  real work to the handler.
end note

note over SHH
  ROLE: The real engine.
  Owns the connection pool keyed by
  (scheme, host, port). Serialises
  HTTP. Reuses TCP connections.
  Crosses into the OS via P/Invoke.
end note

note over SOCK
  ROLE: Syscall boundary.
  socket(), connect(), send(), recv().
  Allocates fd, port, kernel buffers.
  Enforces ring 3 -> ring 0 transition.
end note

note over STACK
  ROLE: Protocol implementation.
  Wraps data: TCP header -> IP header
  -> Ethernet frame (encapsulation).
end note

note over DRV
  ROLE: Hardware translation.
  Writes frame to DMA ring buffer,
  signals the NIC, handles interrupts.
end note

note over NIC
  ROLE: Physical conversion.
  Bytes -> electrical / optical signals.
  Not OS — pure hardware.
end note

' ===================== FLOW: REQUEST =====================
== 1. Application issues request ==
APP -> HC : PostAsync(url, jsonBody)
note right of APP #E6F1FB
  HttpRequestMessage created:
  method=POST, headers, body string.
end note

HC -> SHH : SendAsync(request)
note right of HC #E6F1FB
  Wrapper hands the request to the
  handler pipeline. Nothing OS-level
  has happened yet.
end note

== 2. Connection pool check (the critical step) ==
SHH -> SHH : lookup idle connection\nfor (https, host, 443)
alt connection exists in pool (handler reused)
    note over SHH #EAF3DE
      HIT: reuse existing TCP+TLS
      connection. Skip steps 3-6
      entirely. ~0ms setup cost.
    end note
else no connection (new HttpClient per call)
    note over SHH #FCEBEB
      MISS: must build a brand-new
      connection. This is the path
      taken on EVERY call when you
      do new HttpClient() per request.
    end note

    == 3. DNS resolution ==
    SHH -> SOCK : getaddrinfo("api.example.com")
    SOCK -> STACK : resolve hostname
    STACK --> SOCK : 203.0.113.42
    SOCK --> SHH : IP address
    note right of SOCK #FAEEDA
      DNS result cached in the handler.
      .NET ignores TTL — long-lived
      clients risk stale DNS.
    end note

    == 4. Socket creation ==
    SHH -> SOCK : socket() + connect()
    note over SOCK #FAEEDA
      Kernel allocates:
      - file descriptor (fd=7)
      - TCP control block (TCB)
      - ephemeral port (e.g. 54231)
      - send/recv buffers (~87KB each)
      Updates port -> process table.
    end note

    == 5. TCP 3-way handshake ==
    SOCK -> STACK : initiate handshake
    STACK -> NIC : SYN
    NIC -> SRV : SYN (on wire)
    SRV --> NIC : SYN-ACK
    NIC --> STACK : SYN-ACK
    STACK -> NIC : ACK
    NIC -> SRV : ACK
    note over STACK, SRV #FAEEDA
      Connection state: ESTABLISHED.
      Costs 1 full round trip (~20-100ms).
    end note

    == 6. TLS handshake (HTTPS) ==
    SHH -> TLS : authenticate
    TLS -> SRV : ClientHello
    SRV --> TLS : ServerHello + Certificate
    TLS -> SRV : key exchange
    SRV --> TLS : Finished
    note over TLS, SRV #FAEEDA
      1-2 more round trips. Session keys
      derived. Channel now encrypted.
      Windows: Schannel. Linux: OpenSSL.
    end note
end

== 7. Serialise + send HTTP bytes ==
SHH -> TLS : write HTTP request bytes
note right of SHH #E6F1FB
  "POST /v1/driver HTTP/1.1\r\n
   Host: api.example.com\r\n
   Content-Type: application/json\r\n
   \r\n
   {"driverId":"abc123"}"
end note
TLS -> SOCK : encrypted bytes -> send()

== 8. Encapsulation (each layer adds a header) ==
SOCK -> STACK : hand bytes to TCP layer
note over STACK #FAEEDA
  TCP:      + src port 54231, dst port 443,
            seq, ack, checksum
  IP:       + src IP, dst IP, TTL, proto=6
  Ethernet: + src MAC, dst MAC (router!),
            FCS checksum
  Result: [Eth][IP][TCP][TLS][HTTP][FCS]
end note

== 9. Hand frame to NIC driver ==
STACK -> DRV : dev_queue_xmit(skb)
note right of STACK #EAF3DE
  This is a direct C function call,
  NOT a message. Same memory space —
  a pointer to the buffer is passed.
end note

== 10. DMA to hardware ==
DRV -> NIC : write frame to DMA ring buffer\nsignal "frame ready"
note over DRV, NIC #FAEEDA
  NIC reads bytes from RAM via DMA —
  CPU not involved in the copy.
end note

== 11. Out onto the wire ==
NIC -> SRV : serialise bytes -> electrical/optical signals
note over NIC #F1EFE8
  1 Gbps = 1 billion voltage changes/sec.
  Travels to router, then across internet.
end note

' ===================== FLOW: RESPONSE =====================
== 12. Response — the entire path reverses ==
SRV -> NIC : response signals on wire
NIC -> DRV : DMA write to RX ring\n+ raise hardware interrupt
note over NIC, DRV #FAEEDA
  CPU pauses current work, runs the
  interrupt handler to receive data.
end note
DRV -> STACK : pass frame up
STACK -> STACK : strip Ethernet -> IP -> TCP headers
STACK -> SOCK : deliver payload to socket buffer
SOCK -> TLS : encrypted bytes
TLS -> SHH : decrypted HTTP response bytes
SHH -> HC : HttpResponseMessage
HC --> APP : return response
note right of APP #E6F1FB
  Your ReadAsync() / await completes.
  You get status code + headers + body.
end note

' ===================== DISPOSAL =====================
== 13. Disposal — where the leak happens ==
alt handler reused (IHttpClientFactory)
    note over SHH #EAF3DE
      Connection returned to pool,
      kept alive for the next request.
      No socket closed. No TIME_WAIT.
    end note
else new HttpClient() abandoned per call
    APP -> SOCK : (eventually) GC finalizer -> close(fd)
    note over SOCK #FCEBEB
      Socket enters TIME_WAIT (~60-120s).
      Port unavailable for reuse.
      At high req/s -> port exhaustion
      -> SocketException.
    end note
end

@enduml
